WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:00.630 --> 00:00:05.210
There are a lot of pivotal movie scenes that
really require thespians to crank up the waterworks

00:00:05.210 --> 00:00:07.590
and send home the emotion of the moment.

00:00:07.590 --> 00:00:12.510
So, how do these stars manage to trickle out
those tears again and again with each take?

00:00:12.510 --> 00:00:16.060
Let's take a look at how some of Hollywood's
finest make it happen.

00:00:17.700 --> 00:00:21.680
It may not be common, but there are some actors
who have the ability to summon up authentic

00:00:21.689 --> 00:00:24.749
sobs by sheer force of will, and lots of practice.

00:00:24.749 --> 00:00:28.839
Bryce Dallas Howard famously demonstrated
her natural gift for crying on command during

00:00:28.839 --> 00:00:32.720
an interview with Conan O'Brien, shedding
tears while listening to the talk show host

00:00:32.720 --> 00:00:36.680
tell a nonsensical story about the variety
of duct tape at Home Depot:

00:00:37.600 --> 00:00:38.100
"Duct tape.

00:00:38.340 --> 00:00:39.410
All kinds of duct tape.

00:00:39.410 --> 00:00:40.730
You can get grey duct tape."

00:00:40.730 --> 00:00:41.220
"Yeah."

00:00:41.220 --> 00:00:42.720
"You do this incredible thing.

00:00:42.720 --> 00:00:44.560
You give so much and you get so little in
return."

00:00:44.560 --> 00:00:45.060
"I know."

00:00:45.660 --> 00:00:49.360
The actress later returned to the same show
and revealed that her teary talent is far

00:00:49.360 --> 00:00:50.200
from genetic.

00:00:50.280 --> 00:00:51.280
"It’s technical."

00:00:51.360 --> 00:00:51.860
"It's technical"

00:00:51.860 --> 00:00:56.040
"It’s something that can be learned, and
taught, and you just need to give me a little

00:00:56.040 --> 00:00:56.540
bit of money…"

00:00:56.540 --> 00:00:57.040
"Right."

00:00:57.040 --> 00:00:59.360
"... and I’ll give you all the secrets."

00:01:00.080 --> 00:01:04.240
According to Howard, inducing the soft palate
lift that comes with yawning again and again

00:01:04.259 --> 00:01:07.170
is key in helping her produce those crocodile
tears.

00:01:07.170 --> 00:01:08.170
The actress revealed:

00:01:08.170 --> 00:01:11.520
"You do it enough time and when you're talking
you can continue to lift it.

00:01:11.520 --> 00:01:15.270
It triggers it, just talking about it for
me, it's like a physiological thing."

00:01:15.270 --> 00:01:19.310
Howard also added that the biggest key to
crying on cue is making sure to stay completely

00:01:19.310 --> 00:01:22.760
hydrated so there's plenty of H2O to spare
in her system.

00:01:23.220 --> 00:01:27.400
Amy Adams can also turn on the sprinklers
at a moment's notice, but instead of relying

00:01:27.400 --> 00:01:31.869
on a physical trick, the actress claims that
she uses quite a spicy memory to make the

00:01:31.869 --> 00:01:32.560
waterworks happen.

00:01:32.560 --> 00:01:37.100
Adams told Jimmy Fallon that her daughter
helped coax her into crying on cue by telling

00:01:37.100 --> 00:01:41.079
her about a Sriracha plant in California that
was shuttered after neighboring residents

00:01:41.080 --> 00:01:43.860
complained about the fumes coming from the
production facility.

00:01:45.040 --> 00:01:46.480
"I really like Sriracha.

00:01:47.300 --> 00:01:51.000
And, umm, I don't know if you heard, but they
were thinking of closing the… the plant."

00:01:51.740 --> 00:01:53.240
Okay, maybe it was a put on.

00:01:53.440 --> 00:01:56.420
But just try and name a bigger Sriracha fan,
we dare you!

00:01:56.780 --> 00:02:01.020
Anna Faris revealed to late-night host James
Corden that she isn't the type of person who

00:02:01.029 --> 00:02:04.939
can easily sob in real life, meaning it's
that much more difficult for her to cry on

00:02:04.940 --> 00:02:05.440
camera.

00:02:05.440 --> 00:02:06.500
The actress said:

00:02:06.500 --> 00:02:07.920
"It's actually… a little bit weird.

00:02:07.940 --> 00:02:11.100
If I ever cried in my personal life, [my mom]
would sort of come down on me, like, 'What

00:02:11.100 --> 00:02:12.460
do you have to feel sorry for?'”

00:02:12.640 --> 00:02:16.390
So, instead of trying to power through with
real tears, Faris relies on a device called

00:02:16.390 --> 00:02:17.140
a tear stick.

00:02:17.390 --> 00:02:22.040
Faris, Corden, and fellow guest Josh Jackson
each agreed to have the menthol crystal vapors

00:02:22.040 --> 00:02:25.720
blown into their eyes to demonstrate for the
crowd, and within seconds, they were each

00:02:25.720 --> 00:02:26.540
dripping with tears.

00:02:26.540 --> 00:02:30.220
That's when Jackson revealed the secondary
trick to selling these fake tears on-screen:

00:02:30.840 --> 00:02:32.920
"Now look three-quarters away and slightly
up."

00:02:33.280 --> 00:02:33.780
"Yeah."

00:02:37.640 --> 00:02:38.240
Nailed it.

00:02:38.640 --> 00:02:42.780
Some actors don't have to stretch their minds
too far to work up the requisite tears for

00:02:42.780 --> 00:02:43.420
a tough scene.

00:02:43.480 --> 00:02:47.700
Daniel Kaluuya revealed that while filming
the stand-out "Sunken Place" scene for Jordan

00:02:47.700 --> 00:02:51.900
Peele's Get Out, in which his character was
physically frozen in fear, he simply thought

00:02:51.900 --> 00:02:55.240
about what it would be like to be in a similar
situation in real life.

00:02:55.240 --> 00:02:59.450
In an “Actors on Actors” chat for Variety,
Kaluuya explained that a large part of tapping

00:02:59.450 --> 00:03:04.380
into that headspace to cry involved recognizing
the parallels between his character's situation

00:03:04.380 --> 00:03:06.060
and everyday scenarios, saying:

00:03:06.060 --> 00:03:09.910
"It's just empathizing with someone that's
going through so much and has suppressed it,

00:03:09.910 --> 00:03:13.100
kind of suppressed his guilt of a really bad
situation and shame.

00:03:13.100 --> 00:03:14.840
And also his reluctance to face it.

00:03:15.000 --> 00:03:17.320
He's paralyzed, he's paralyzed emotionally.

00:03:17.320 --> 00:03:19.400
And I identified with that in all walks of
life.”

00:03:19.480 --> 00:03:22.500
And thus, an unforgettable cinematic crying
scene was born.

00:03:29.660 --> 00:03:31.180
"Now you're in the sunken place."

00:03:32.320 --> 00:03:36.540
Empathy is a valuable trait and certainly
helpful in crying on cue, but if an actor

00:03:36.541 --> 00:03:40.710
has his or her own harrowing life experience
to help bring the sad sobs to the set, it's

00:03:40.710 --> 00:03:41.340
even better.

00:03:41.340 --> 00:03:45.160
For one of his first films after his Harry
Potter days, Daniel Radcliffe starred as the

00:03:45.160 --> 00:03:49.490
beat poet Allen Ginsberg in the biographical
drama-thriller Kill Your Darlings.

00:03:49.490 --> 00:03:53.100
The role required Radcliffe to shed a lot
of tears, something that wasn’t exactly

00:03:53.100 --> 00:03:54.540
easy for the actor to do.

00:03:54.540 --> 00:03:56.260
Radcliffe told the Vancouver Sun:

00:03:56.260 --> 00:03:59.920
"Unless you have that trick of being able
to cry on cue, which some people can do, the

00:03:59.920 --> 00:04:03.640
more you try to force it, the more awkward
and awful it becomes… as I have found!"

00:04:03.640 --> 00:04:06.360
Unfortunately, there’s no magic spell to
summon tears.

00:04:06.370 --> 00:04:10.490
However, Radcliffe revealed that he revisited
a helpful pointer from Gary Oldman to help

00:04:10.490 --> 00:04:11.900
him turn on the waterworks.

00:04:11.900 --> 00:04:14.880
The actor said his former on-screen godfather
once told him:

00:04:14.880 --> 00:04:18.160
"Don't be afraid to use all your own stuff
because they're only going to see it happening

00:04:18.160 --> 00:04:19.080
to the character."

00:04:19.220 --> 00:04:21.220
"You weren't gonna tell me?"

00:04:24.000 --> 00:04:25.740
"We both know why you can't come."

00:04:29.600 --> 00:04:33.530
Radcliffe didn't share what memory did the
trick exactly, but revealed that he did find

00:04:33.530 --> 00:04:34.980
some success with it, saying:

00:04:35.040 --> 00:04:39.480
"Three uncontrollable, breaking-down scenes
later… there were these real, raw tears.

00:04:39.490 --> 00:04:43.350
The first time that happens to you on a set
full of people… even if you go in just as

00:04:43.350 --> 00:04:45.700
that character, at that moment, it is just
you.”

00:04:45.860 --> 00:04:48.300
Now, that’s a magical moment if we’ve
ever heard one.

00:04:48.820 --> 00:04:53.020
Without a built-in trick or handy dandy device
to help bring on the waterworks, some actors

00:04:53.030 --> 00:04:57.460
just have to jump into an emotional scene
feet first and hope their eyes catch up somewhere

00:04:57.460 --> 00:04:58.180
along the way.

00:04:58.260 --> 00:05:02.000
Oscar winner Jennifer Lawrence revealed that
for one of her big crying scenes, she actually

00:05:02.000 --> 00:05:05.840
went through the bodily motions of mourning,
and the tears happened to follow suit.

00:05:05.840 --> 00:05:07.350
The actress told MTV News:

00:05:07.350 --> 00:05:10.200
"I can definitely start [sobbing] and water
comes, sometimes.

00:05:10.200 --> 00:05:13.340
I don't have anything in my life that can
instantly make me cry.

00:05:13.340 --> 00:05:16.440
It's mostly just the standard, hold your eyes
open, fake your way through it."

00:05:16.660 --> 00:05:20.870
Lawrence later elaborated that she needs to
widen her eyes for a "long time" to make her

00:05:20.870 --> 00:05:23.620
method work, but that it’s still her go-to
for the gushy scenes.

00:05:23.620 --> 00:05:27.960
Considering how stacked her trophy shelf is
right about now, whatever she's doing is obviously

00:05:27.960 --> 00:05:29.120
working just fine.

00:05:29.480 --> 00:05:33.580
Will Smith might have no trouble serving up
serious drama now that he's such a seasoned

00:05:33.580 --> 00:05:37.710
film star, but back in his earliest days on
The Fresh Prince of Bel-Air, Smith was still

00:05:37.710 --> 00:05:39.460
learning how to emote on camera.

00:05:39.650 --> 00:05:44.120
His starring sitcom role almost always relied
on his skill for being suave and self-assured,

00:05:44.120 --> 00:05:47.730
but there was one important moment in the
show's fourth season that required the actor

00:05:47.730 --> 00:05:48.760
to really dig deep.

00:05:48.990 --> 00:05:53.380
In the poignant episode, Will's father returns
to his life and gives the teen hope that he

00:05:53.380 --> 00:05:57.100
might finally forge a bond with the man who's
been M.I.A. throughout his upbringing.

00:05:57.220 --> 00:06:01.640
However, he doesn’t stick around for long,
and Will delivers a heartbreaking monologue

00:06:01.640 --> 00:06:04.740
that vacillates between contempt, determination,
and a lot of hurt.

00:06:07.220 --> 00:06:08.780
"How come he don’t want me, man?"

00:06:15.360 --> 00:06:19.880
Without a strong delivery by Smith, the scene
might've been hokey, but he sold his sadness

00:06:19.880 --> 00:06:22.490
thanks to the help of his on-screen uncle,
James Avery.

00:06:22.490 --> 00:06:23.200
Smith revealed:

00:06:23.200 --> 00:06:25.760
"James Avery was relentlessly on me to elevate.

00:06:25.760 --> 00:06:29.650
He was like, 'You have this position, look
where you are, look what you are blessed with.'

00:06:29.650 --> 00:06:33.940
He was like, 'I'm sorry but I'm not accepting
anything other than absolute committed perfection

00:06:33.940 --> 00:06:34.460
from you.'"

00:06:34.920 --> 00:06:39.040
Hollywood history is filled with stories of
filmmakers using near-torturous techniques

00:06:39.050 --> 00:06:43.130
to elicit an emotional response from their
stars, like when Alfred Hitchcock tormented

00:06:43.130 --> 00:06:48.020
Tippi Hedren on the set of The Birds to capture
real fear or when Stanley Kubrick made Shelley

00:06:48.020 --> 00:06:52.810
Duvall unwell with his harsh demands on the
set of The Shining, and her condition conveniently

00:06:52.810 --> 00:06:54.120
aligned with her character's.

00:06:55.920 --> 00:06:58.020
"I'm very confused…

00:06:59.720 --> 00:07:02.840
I just need a chance to think things over."

00:07:04.000 --> 00:07:08.419
Similarly, there have been several noteworthy
cases of cast members being coerced into crying

00:07:08.419 --> 00:07:11.720
on-set by some of their co-workers, whether
they liked it or not.

00:07:11.720 --> 00:07:16.050
For example, when he was still a child star,
Jackie Cooper was bullied by director Norman

00:07:16.050 --> 00:07:18.940
Taurog into bringing it with his tears in
Skippy.

00:07:18.940 --> 00:07:22.810
Though the role earned the then-nine-year-old
an Oscar nomination, it’s safe to say that

00:07:22.810 --> 00:07:26.550
Taurog’s threat to kill Cooper’s dog if
the actor didn’t cry took things a bit too

00:07:26.550 --> 00:07:27.040
far.

00:07:27.040 --> 00:07:32.300
Similarly, Francis Ford Coppola reportedly
resorted to calling Winona Ryder vicious names

00:07:32.300 --> 00:07:35.440
to bring her to tears on the set of Bram Stoker's
Dracula.

00:07:36.100 --> 00:07:39.860
There are some actors so talented that they
simply don’t have a need to turn to methods

00:07:39.860 --> 00:07:43.300
or manufacturing when they’re called to
be emotionally affected in a scene.

00:07:43.300 --> 00:07:47.889
In a surprise to no one, Meryl Streep, largely
considered the greatest actress of our time,

00:07:47.889 --> 00:07:49.270
is one of those skilled few.

00:07:49.270 --> 00:07:53.650
In a discussion with the students at the University
of Texas, Streep revealed that her process

00:07:53.650 --> 00:07:57.780
is simply to power through whatever challenge
she faces, including bringing tears to the

00:07:57.780 --> 00:08:00.400
table even when she isn't actually in the
mopey mood.

00:08:00.400 --> 00:08:01.320
The actress said:

00:08:01.520 --> 00:08:04.220
"The hardest thing to do is to do something
when you don't feel it.

00:08:04.220 --> 00:08:07.640
It's weird and hard to do what isn't really
happening and make it like it is.

00:08:07.650 --> 00:08:08.669
But we need actors.

00:08:08.669 --> 00:08:11.780
We need them to help us understand different
emotions and realities.

00:08:11.780 --> 00:08:12.780
To console us.

00:08:12.780 --> 00:08:14.040
To let us know that we're not alone."

00:08:14.240 --> 00:08:18.180
In other words, whenever Streep needs to cry
on screen, she simply remembers how important

00:08:18.180 --> 00:08:20.900
it is for Meryl Streep to cry on screen, and
she gets the job done.

00:08:20.900 --> 00:08:25.200
That kind of gumption just can't be taught,
so it's no wonder she's so many light-years

00:08:25.200 --> 00:08:26.200
ahead of the rest.

00:08:26.680 --> 00:08:31.140
Oprah Winfrey seems to be good at everything,
but when the TV mogul does occasionally dip

00:08:31.150 --> 00:08:34.960
her toes into the waters of Hollywood to star
in a movie, it's not always easy for her.

00:08:34.960 --> 00:08:39.080
In fact, Winfrey told Larry King that before
she earned an Academy Award nomination for

00:08:39.080 --> 00:08:42.580
her work in The Color Purple, she felt like
a failure because she could only cry when

00:08:42.580 --> 00:08:44.920
the cameras were pointed elsewhere during
production.

00:08:45.040 --> 00:08:48.000
As soon as they were facing her, the tears
were nowhere to be found.

00:08:48.360 --> 00:08:51.520
"So all night long I cried, because I couldn’t
cry."

00:08:52.080 --> 00:08:56.340
Winfrey later revealed that before she began
working on Lee Daniels' The Butler, she decided

00:08:56.350 --> 00:09:00.200
to bring in some help and learn how to fake
some of that raw emotion that came so easily

00:09:00.200 --> 00:09:01.640
on her daytime talk show.

00:09:01.640 --> 00:09:02.300
Winfrey said:

00:09:02.320 --> 00:09:05.140
"Everybody thinks that I'm a good crier, an
easy crier.

00:09:05.149 --> 00:09:09.070
I can cry if I'm in the moment and feeling
emotion, but as an actress, that was one of

00:09:09.070 --> 00:09:09.940
my biggest fears.”

00:09:09.940 --> 00:09:13.860
Thanks to the enlistment of an acting coach,
Winfrey said she figured out how to make herself

00:09:13.860 --> 00:09:15.280
cry in just 20 minutes.

00:09:15.280 --> 00:09:17.020
Now, that’s something to jump up and down
about.

00:09:17.600 --> 00:09:21.700
The late, great Shirley Temple might have
been just a tiny tyke when she became a legend

00:09:21.700 --> 00:09:25.060
of the early screen scene, but even then she
was a consummate professional.

00:09:25.060 --> 00:09:29.740
In a 1999 interview the former child superstar
revealed her method for breaking out with

00:09:29.740 --> 00:09:31.150
a wail on camera, saying:

00:09:31.150 --> 00:09:33.000
"I guess I was an early method actress.

00:09:33.000 --> 00:09:36.000
I would go to a quiet part of the soundstage
with my mother.

00:09:36.000 --> 00:09:39.320
I wouldn't think of anything sad, I would
just make my mind a blank.

00:09:39.320 --> 00:09:40.390
In a minute I could cry."

00:09:40.390 --> 00:09:42.700
Temple did have one crying caveat, though.

00:09:42.700 --> 00:09:46.529
The Hollywood legend said she didn’t like
to cry after lunch, because she was too content.

00:09:46.529 --> 00:09:49.480
Who can be sad after chowing down on animal
crackers in their soup?

